// LeetCode 279 — Perfect Squares.
//
// Given `n`, return the least number of perfect squares that sum to it.
// `12 = 4 + 4 + 4` is 3; `13 = 4 + 9` is 2.
//
// THE ★ MECHANISM: BOTTOM-UP DP. `least[i]` is the answer for `i`, built by
// trying every square that fits:
//
//     least[0] = 0
//     least[i] = 1 + min over j*j <= i of least[i - j*j]
//
// O(n√n) time, O(n) space. The recurrence is the definition read backwards: any
// optimal decomposition of `i` ends in SOME square `j*j`, and what remains must
// itself be optimally decomposed — otherwise you could improve it and contradict
// optimality. That exchange argument is the whole proof, and it is why a greedy
// "take the largest square that fits" is wrong: for 12 greedy takes 9, leaving
// 3 = 1+1+1, for a total of 4 against the true 3.
//
// GREEDY FAILING AT 12 IS WORTH KNOWING because it is the first counterexample
// and it is small. A solver tested only on 13 (where greedy is right) passes.
//
// THE ANSWER IS ALWAYS 1, 2, 3, OR 4 — Lagrange's four-square theorem — which
// the DP never discovers and never needs. `perfect_squares_theory.kara` computes
// it from that fact alone in O(√n) with no table and no search, and the
// differential leans on the two agreeing: a DP bug and a number-theory bug have
// no way to coincide.

fn least_squares(n: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut least: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    least.push(0);
    let mut i = 1;
    while i <= n {
        let mut best = i;                 // worst case: i copies of 1
        let mut j = 1;
        while j * j <= i {
            let cand = least[i - j * j] + 1;
            if cand < best { best = cand; }
            j = j + 1;
        }
        least.push(best);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return least[n];
}

fn main() {
    let cases: Vec[i64] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 12, 13, 43, 48,
                           99, 100, 9999];
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < cases.len() {
        let n = cases[i];
        println(f"n={n} -> {least_squares(n)}");
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
